3D logic‑to‑DRAM process-near‑memory (L2D‑PNM) architecture delivers high on‑chip bandwidth and bank‑level parallelism by vertically stacking a logic die with multiple DRAM dies, effectively alleviating the memory wall problem in DNN inference. However, such 3D integration causes high power density and central hotspots that critically constrain hardware performance and reliability. To address this, we propose the first thermal‑aware scheduling framework for DNN inference on 3D L2D‑PNM architectures, which mitigates thermal-throttling-induced performance degradation and aims to minimize inference latency under temperature constraints.